<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lullaby · date unknown · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Tinsagu nu Hana</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ryu">てぃんさぐぬ花</bdi> / <bdi lang="ryu-Latn">Tinsagu nu Hana</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A children's song from Okinawa, named after the balsam flower, that teaches children to honour what their parents tell them.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Okinawan (Ryukyuan)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Okinawan</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Okinawa, Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional; date not known</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional Okinawan song; authors not known)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinsagu-nu-hana">Tinsagu nu Hana</a>&quot;, also &quot;Chinsagu nu Hana&quot;, is an Okinawan children's song in the Okinawan language. Its title can be translated as &quot;The Balsam Flowers&quot;: Okinawan children would squeeze the sap from balsam flowers to stain their fingernails, as a way to ward off evil. Its words are Confucian teachings, of the traditional Ryukyuan values such as filial piety. Of its first six verses, the first three are about filial piety and the last three about respecting one's body and one's goals. Every verse has exactly the same number of notes, in the language and metre that are uniquely Okinawan and are called ryūka. The song has ten verses, but the last four are missing from the text usually printed.</p><p>On 18 March 2012, after a public survey, the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-prefecture">Okinawa Prefecture</a> designated &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinsagu-nu-hana">Tinsagu nu Hana</a>&quot; as &quot;Okinawa Prefecture's favorite song&quot;. It then became an official symbol of the prefecture, its second musical symbol beside the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Okinawa Prefecture&quot;.</p><p>The song has also travelled beyond Okinawa. Ryuichi Sakamoto used it on his album Beauty; according to Felicity Collins of La Trobe University, this was the first time the song was &quot;appropriated for a 'world music' audience&quot;. It is heard at the end of the Australian film Japanese Story (2003), sung by Shelley Scown with orchestral work by Elizabeth Drake, as the heroine, Sandy, watches the aircraft that carries the body of Hiromitsu Tachibana, a Japanese man she has come to know, taxi and prepare to take off. Collins wrote that some Japanese critics found this use contentious: the song has a specifically Okinawan character and is not something generally associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and the fragility and beauty of a song about upholding Okinawan identity seemed to them out of place in an Australian film.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of moral teaching for children. Each verse sets an image beside a lesson: as balsam juice colours the fingertips, a parent's teaching should colour the heart; the stars of the sky can be counted, but not a parent's teachings; a ship at night steers by the north star as a child is guided by its parents; a jewel left unpolished tarnishes, so a person must work on themselves every day; the sincere will see their wishes come true; and anything can be done if one tries. The mood is gentle and earnest.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Okinawan</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Okinawan</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">てぃんさぐぬ花や</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Tinsagu nu hana ya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Just as my fingernails</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">爪先に染みてぃ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Chimisachi ni sumiti</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are stained with the pigment from balsam flowers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">親ぬゆし事や</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Uya nu yushigutu ya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My heart is painted</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">肝に染みり</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Chimu ni sumiri</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With the teachings of my parents</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">天ぬ群星や</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Tin nu muribushi ya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Although the stars in the sky</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">読みば読まりしが</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Yumiba yumarishi ga</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are countable</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">親ぬゆし言や</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Uya nu yushigutu ya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The teachings of my parents</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">読みやならん</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Yumiyanaran</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are not</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">夜走らす舟や</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Yuru harasu funi ya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Just as ships that run in the night</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">子ぬ方星見当てぃ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Ninufabushi miati</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are guided to safety by the North Star</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">我ん生ちぇる親や</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Wan nacheru uya ya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I am guided by my parents</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">我んどぅ見当てぃ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Wan du miati</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who gave birth to me and watch over me</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">宝玉やてぃん</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Takaradama yati n</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There's no point in possessing magnificent jewelry</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">磨かにば錆す</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Migakaniba sabisu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If you don't maintain it</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">朝夕肝磨ち</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Asayu chimu migachi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>People who maintain their bodies</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">浮世渡ら</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Uchi yu watara</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will live life wonderfully</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">誠する人や</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Makutu suru hitu ya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The desires of the person who lives sincerely</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">後や何時迄ん</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Atu ya ichi madi n</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will always run true</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">思事ん叶てぃ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Umukutu n kanati</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And as a result</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">千代ぬ栄い</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Chiyu nu sakai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She will prosper</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">なしば何事ん</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Nashiba nangutu n</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You can do anything</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">なゆる事やしが</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Nayurugutu yashi ga</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If you try</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">なさぬ故からどぅ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Nasanu yui kara du</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But you can't</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ryu">ならぬ定み</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ryu-Latn">Naranu sadami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If you don't</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional Okinawan; six of the ten verses, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tinsagu_nu_Hana" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>Romanized: Transliteration as given in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tinsagu_nu_Hana" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: English translation from Wikipedia's article (CC BY-SA). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tinsagu_nu_Hana" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
